1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:06,684
Open Data Lake, then eth lake, and click the blocks table.

2
00:00:06,964 --> 00:00:09,161
Studio opens the table's own page.

3
00:00:13,572 --> 00:00:17,943
The Schema tab lists the columns and their types, as the catalog stores them.

4
00:00:18,893 --> 00:00:21,915
And Data Files: three Parquet files, one per load.

5
00:00:22,195 --> 00:00:28,751
August, September, and October, each about seventy megabytes, with its row count and the snapshot that added it.

6
00:00:29,701 --> 00:00:31,987
The same list is one function in SQL.

7
00:00:32,267 --> 00:00:38,344
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake, ducklake list files gives each data file of the table, and its size.

8
00:00:41,735 --> 00:00:43,072
The files are on your disk.

9
00:00:43,352 --> 00:00:49,943
Glob lists every Parquet file under the lake's data folder: the same three, in a folder per schema and table.

10
00:00:50,893 --> 00:00:52,266
And they are plain Parquet.

11
00:00:52,546 --> 00:00:57,738
Read them directly, without the lake, and you get the same six hundred sixty thousand rows.

12
00:00:58,688 --> 00:01:01,763
So any tool that reads Parquet can read these files.

13
00:01:02,713 --> 00:01:06,271
But only the catalog knows which files make up the table right now.

14
00:01:06,551 --> 00:01:12,813
After a delete or an update, the folder also holds files that are no longer current, and delete files.

15
00:01:13,093 --> 00:01:16,015
Reading the folder directly would give the wrong answer.

16
00:01:16,295 --> 00:01:18,030
That is what the catalog is for.

17
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:25,108
Save the queries as sql, 2 underscore 3 look inside dot sequel, then commit, and push.

18
00:01:46,553 --> 00:01:50,738
To recap: Studio's table page shows the schema and the data files.

19
00:01:51,018 --> 00:01:56,451
In SQL, ducklake list files gives the same list, and the files are plain Parquet on disk.

20
00:01:56,731 --> 00:01:59,420
But the catalog decides which files are the table.

21
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:00,964
Next: snapshots.
